computeIfAbsent() is a very useful method in Map.

Syntax
map.computeIfAbsent(key, k -> value);

It means:

"If the key is not present, create a value using the lambda and put it into the map.
Otherwise, return the existing value."

Example 1

Without computeIfAbsent

Map<String, Integer> map = new HashMap<>();

if (!map.containsKey("A")) {
    map.put("A", 1);
}

System.out.println(map);

Output:

{A=1}
Same thing using computeIfAbsent
Map<String, Integer> map = new HashMap<>();

map.computeIfAbsent("A", k -> 1);

System.out.println(map);

Output:

{A=1}
Example 2 (Most common use case)

For Graphs:

    Map<Integer, List<Integer>> graph = new HashMap<>();

    graph.computeIfAbsent(1, k -> new ArrayList<>()).add(2);
    graph.computeIfAbsent(1, k -> new ArrayList<>()).add(3);

    First line
    graph.computeIfAbsent(1, k -> new ArrayList<>())

Map:

{}

    Key 1 doesn't exist. So Java does:

    graph.put(1, new ArrayList<>());

    Returns: []

Then:
    .add(2);

    Map becomes:

    {1=[2]}

Second line
    graph.computeIfAbsent(1, k -> new ArrayList<>())

Map:

    {1=[2]}

    Key 1 already exists. So Java:

    DOES NOT create a new ArrayList
    Returns existing [2]

Then:
    .add(3);

    Map becomes:

    {1=[2,3]}

    Equivalent code
    if (!graph.containsKey(1)) {
        graph.put(1, new ArrayList<>());
    }

graph.get(1).add(2);

computeIfAbsent() is just a shorter version of this pattern.

Another example
Map<Character, List<Integer>> map = new HashMap<>();

    String s = "banana";

    for (int i = 0; i < s.length(); i++) {

        char ch = s.charAt(i);

        map.computeIfAbsent(ch, k -> new ArrayList<>()).add(i);
    }

System.out.println(map);

Output:

    {
     a=[1,3,5],
     b=[0],
     n=[2,4]
    }

Important thing to remember
    map.computeIfAbsent(key, k -> value)

    means:

    IF key is absent
        create value
        put into map
    RETURN the value associated with the key
    Difference from putIfAbsent
    map.putIfAbsent(key, value);

Only inserts.

It returns the old value.

You still need:

map.get(key)

afterward.

computeIfAbsent() is preferred when the value is a collection:

Map<Integer, List<Integer>>
Map<Character, Set<String>>
Map<String, PriorityQueue<Integer>>

because you can do:

map.computeIfAbsent(key, k -> new ArrayList<>()).add(value);

in a single line.

if  value is present

computeIfPresent() is the opposite of computeIfAbsent().

Syntax
map.computeIfPresent(key, (k, v) -> newValue);

    It means:

    "If the key is already present, compute a new value and update the map."
    If the key is not present, nothing happens.

Example 1
    Map<String, Integer> map = new HashMap<>();

    map.put("A", 10);

    map.computeIfPresent("A", (k, v) -> v + 5);

    System.out.println(map);

Output:

    {A=15}

    Explanation:

    k = "A"
    v = 10

    new value = 10 + 5 = 15

Map becomes:

{A=15}

Example 2
    Map<String, Integer> map = new HashMap<>();

    map.computeIfPresent("A", (k, v) -> v + 5);

    System.out.println(map);

Output:

    {}

Nothing happens because "A" doesn't exist.

Most common example

Count frequencies:

Map<Character, Integer> freq = new HashMap<>();

for (char ch : str.toCharArray()) {

    freq.putIfAbsent(ch, 0);

    freq.computeIfPresent(ch, (k, v) -> v + 1);
}

Another example :-

Map<String, Integer> salary = new HashMap<>();

    salary.put("John", 1000);

    salary.computeIfPresent(
            "John",
            (name, amount) -> amount + 500
    );

System.out.println(salary);

Output:

{John=1500}

Removing an entry with computeIfPresent

    If the lambda returns null, the key is removed.

    map.computeIfPresent("A", (k, v) -> null); // if added null then the entry will be removed

    Equivalent to : map.remove("A");

Comparison
    Method	When executed?
    computeIfAbsent()	Key does NOT exist
    computeIfPresent()	Key already exists

Example :-
    Map<Integer, String> map = new HashMap<>();

    map.computeIfAbsent(1, k -> "One");
    map.computeIfPresent(1, (k, v) -> v + " Updated");

    System.out.println(map);

Output:

    {1=One Updated}
    Easy way to remember
    computeIfAbsent  -> create if missing
    computeIfPresent -> update if existing